<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-lin-span-subspaces">

<title>Discovery guide</title>


<activity xml:id="activity-linear-combinations-refresher">
	<introduction>

		<p>
        Recall or learn that a vector <m>\bfv</m> is a 
        <term>linear combination</term> of vectors 
        <m>\bfv_{1}, \bfv_{2}, \ldots, \bfv_{n}</m> if you can write <m>\bfv</m> as a sum of scalar multiples of
        <m>\bfv_{1},\bfv_{2},\ldots, \bfv_{n}</m>, i.e.
        if you can find scalars <m>c_{1}, \ldots, c_{n}</m> so that
        <md>
            \bfv = c_{1} \bfv_{1} + c_{2} \bfv_{2} + \cdots + c_{n} \bfv_{n}.
        </md>
        These <m>c_{i}</m> are called <term>coefficients</term>, or sometimes <term>weights</term>.
        </p>
        </introduction>

        <statement><p>
            Let <m>\bfv_{1} = \threevec{2}{0}{0}</m> and <m>\bfv_{2} = \threevec{1}{0}{1}</m>.
        </p></statement>

        <task><statement><p>
            Is <m>\bfu = \threevec{5}{0}{7}</m> a linear combination of <m>\bfv_{1}</m> and <m>\bfv_{2}</m>?
        </p></statement></task>

        <task><statement><p>
            Is <m>\bfu = \threevec{5}{1}{7}</m> a linear combination of <m>\bfv_{1}</m> and <m>\bfv_{2}</m>?
        </p></statement></task>

        <task><statement><p>
            Can you identify which vectors in <m>\bbR^{3}</m> can be written as a linear
            combination of <m>\bfv_{1}</m> and <m>\bfv_{2}</m>?
            Which vectors <em>cannot</em> be written as a linear combination of
            <m>\bfv_{1}</m> and <m>\bfv_{2}</m>?
        </p></statement></task>

        <task><statement><p>
            For any vectors that <em>can</em> be written as a linear combination of
            <m>\bfv_{1}</m> and <m>\bfv_{2}</m>, determine which coefficients need to be
            used.
        </p></statement></task>
</activity>        

    <definition><p>
        The collection of <em>all possible linear combinations</em> of a set of vectors
        is called the <term>span</term> of those vectors, denoted
        <md>\spn\{\bfv_{1}, \bfv_{2}, \ldots, \bfv_{n}\} = \{ c_{1} \bfv_{1} + c_{2} \bfv_{2} + \cdots + c_{n} \bfv_{n} \mid c_{i} \in \bbR \}.</md>
    </p></definition>


<activity xml:id="activity-span-as-a-word"> 
    <task><statement><p>
        What is <m>\spn\{\bfv_{1},\bfv_{2}\}</m> for <m>\bfv_{1}</m> and
        <m>\bfv_{2}</m> from <xref ref="activity-linear-combinations-refresher"/>?
        (You already computed it in the previous exercise!
        You're just trying new vocab on.)
    </p></statement></task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-span-geometrically">
    <introduction><p>
        Let <m>\bfu_{1} = \twovec{-1}{1}</m> and <m>\bfu_{2} = \twovec{2}{1}</m>. 
    </p></introduction>

    <task><statement><p>
        What is <m>\spn\{\bfu_{1}\}</m>?
        Draw it on a coordinate plane.
    </p></statement></task>


    <task><statement><p>
        What is <m>\spn\{\bfu_{2}\}</m>?
        Draw it on a coordinate plane 
        (maybe in a different color, if you have it).
    </p></statement></task>


    <task><statement><p>
        What is <m>\spn\{\bfu_{1}, \bfu_{2}\}</m>?
        Describe it in terms of the coordinate plane (may be hard to draw).
    </p></statement></task>


    <task><statement><p>
        The vector <m>\twovec{-3}{0}</m> is in <m>\spn\{\bfu_{1}, \bfu_{2}\}</m>.
        Can you interpret it in the context of the pictures you have drawn?
    </p></statement></task>


    <task><statement><p>
        If <m>\bfu_{3} = \twovec{0}{1}</m>, what is
        <m>\spn\{\bfu_{1},\bfu_{2},\bfu_{3}\}</m>?
        How does it compare to <m>\spn\{\bfu_{1},\bfu_{2}\}</m>?
    </p></statement></task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-span-geometrically-degenerate">
    <introduction><p>
        Let <m>\bfw_{1} = \twovec{3}{1}</m> and 
        <m>\bfw_{2} = \twovec{-6}{2}</m>. 
    </p></introduction>

    <task><statement><p>
        What is <m>\spn\{\bfw_{1}\}</m>?
        Draw it on a coordinate plane.
    </p></statement></task>


    <task><statement><p>
        What is <m>\spn\{\bfw_{2}\}</m>?
        Draw it on a coordinate plane 
        (maybe in a different color, if you have it).
    </p></statement></task>


    <task><statement><p>
        What is <m>\spn\{\bfw_{1}, \bfw_{2}\}</m>?
        Draw it on the coordinate plane.
    </p></statement></task>

    <task><statement><p>
        How does this differ from <m>\spn\{\bfu_{1}, \bfu_2\}</m>
        in <xref ref="activity-span-geometrically"/>? 
        What is it about <m>\bfw_1</m> and <m>\bfw_2</m> 
        that changes the outcome? 
    </p></statement></task>

</activity>

<p>
    One of the major properties of real vectors in <m>\bbR^{n}</m> is that we
    can add them together and scale them the same way we do real numbers.
    If a smaller space inside of <m>\bbR^{n}</m> also has these properties, it
    has a special name.
</p>

<definition xml:id="definition-subspace">
    <statement>A subspace of <m>\bbR^{n}</m> is a subset <m>H</m> of <m>\bbR^{n}</m> that
    satisfies the following three properties:
    <ol marker="(i)">
        <li>
            <p>
            <m>\bfzero</m> is in <m>H</m>.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
            If <m>\bfu</m> and <m>\bfv</m> are in <m>H</m>, then so is
            <m>\bfu + \bfv</m>.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
            If <m>\bfu</m> is in <m>H</m>, then so is <m>r \bfu</m> for any
            real number <m>r</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
	</statement>
</definition>

<activity xml:id="activity-subspaces-in-Rn">

    <introduction>
		<p>
        For each of the following collections of vectors, 
		check each criterion from <xref ref="definition-subspace"/>
		to see if that collection is a subspace.
		
		<ul>
			<li>If you think a criterion is <em>true</em>, 
			<alert>justify it without resorting to specific examples</alert>.</li>
			<li>If you think a criterion is <em>false</em>, 
			<alert>provide an explicit example that demonstrates its failure</alert>.</li>
		</ul>
		
		</p>
	
	</introduction>


	<task><statement><p>
		In <m>\bbR^2</m>:
			<task><statement><p>
				All points on the line <m>y=x</m>.
			</p></statement></task>

			<task><statement><p>
				All points on the line <m>y=x+1</m>.
			</p></statement></task>

			<task><statement><p>
				All points on the circle of radius 1 centered at the origin.
			</p></statement></task>
	</p></statement></task>

	<task><statement><p>
		In <m>\bbR^3</m>:
			<task><statement><p>
				All points on the <m>xy</m>-plane.
			</p></statement></task>

			<task><statement><p>
				The plane parallel to the <m>xy</m>-plane at height <m>z=1</m>.
			</p></statement></task>

			<task><statement><p>
				All column vectors <m>\bfx</m> that satisfy the matrix equation 
				<m>A\bfx=\bfzero</m>, where <m>A</m> is some fixed
				<m>2 \times 3</m> matrix.
			</p></statement></task>
				<m>\spn\left\{ \bfu = \threevec{1}{1}{1}, \bfv = \threevec{3}{2}{-1}\right\}</m>.
			<task><statement><p>

			</p></statement></task>
	</p></statement></task>
</activity>


</worksheet>